Previously, in a Different Life — “The Other Branch”

Every episode opens with a recap of a life the audience never saw. This time, it opens on two girls hiding under a kitchen table during a thunderstorm. One of them reaches up, takes the last peach off a plate, and cuts it in quarters. She gives her sister the smallest piece.

Then the episode starts, and we learn the two women have just met.

For six episodes, the audience has been treating the changing wallpaper as visual noise. In episode seven, one woman walks into the other’s kitchen and stops cold. She recognizes the wallpaper. She remembers helping her mother hang it. The other woman remembers peeling it off to sell the house. They both remember the pencil marks on the wall underneath.

That’s what the inheritance idea seeds for me. Give each branch a few details it carries faithfully and a few it can change. Then let two characters meet in the middle of a history neither has ever seen whole.

The season ends with them opening a third kitchen door. The wallpaper is gone. The height marks are still there. There are three names.